--TEST--
every element of `Core\Cli::arguments` is `tainted`, so a word reaches a grammar sink only through the launderer that names it — spec § 15, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` and `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--ARGS--
a.b
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The qualifier is the third thing § 15's signature says, and the only one no
// runtime assertion can show: a plain `string` is assignable into a `tainted`
// target, so a case that only declared one would pass over a member that had
// dropped the mark. What pins it is the refusal -- the words came from outside
// the program's own text, so `rule:security/sink-predicate`'s grammar sinks are shut to them.
foreach (Core\Cli::arguments() as tainted string $word) {
    Core\Regex\Pattern $pattern = Core\Regex::compile($word);
    echo "unreachable\n";
}

// And the route that is open, one line down: `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`'s launderer for that
// exact sink answers a plain `string`, so a program that means to match on a
// word says so where a reader can grep for it.
foreach (Core\Cli::arguments() as tainted string $word) {
    string $quoted = Core\Regex::quote($word);
    echo $quoted, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:8:55
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
